 the world wars made in  japan  was seen as something that was was cheap  and  inferior and knockoff if you saw a  product that said made in japan  you would think it'd be really low  quality and he  had a goal to set his own path for his  nation  to turn made in japan into something  that meant quality  into a stamp of quality production  and it's worked if you look at a product  that says made in japan  versus another country if they're the  same price you're going to go for the  japanese one because you know that it's  made with quality he went so far as to  write a book  called made in japan to highlight that  process so  every step along the way in his life he  had to set his own path  he had to break free of tradition break  free of the rules that were being  imposed on him  he did in his family by saying no to the  sake business since 1665.  he said no to bulava who wanted to own  the branding  for the products that came out and he  said no to the traditional japanese  manufacturing process  that was poor quality and said i'm going  gonna put this country on my back  imagine the vision i'm gonna put this  country on my back and change the way  manufacturing is done you need to  set your own path for your business not  listen to  the traditional ways that things are  done not listen to the opinions of other  people who are trying to hold you where  you  are trying to hold you back from  becoming the entrepreneur that you can  be  set your own path believe in yourself  triumph  over the little man model success and  continue to believe  for those who watching like the video  subscribe to the channel you can see  more like it  thank you so much for watching i'll see  you soon  make sure to check out my newsletter  you're gonna love it  hi i'm jason j ross fonseca of  riseonline.com and i want to say  evan carmichael's newsletter is  consistent fun  inspiring and it's the only newsletter i  prioritize  and look forward to period
 

